 ## Background
-Ray marching optimization has evolved alongside real-time rendering requirements. Early optimizations focused on hardware-specific memory access patterns, such as cubic memory organization for FPGA-based volume rendering to reduce memory accesses for trilinear interpolation [S3]. As real-time engines matured, shader-level optimizations like conservative density evaluation became necessary to handle complex material graphs in environments lacking dynamic branching [S1]. More recent developments include view-dependent optimizations for Monte Carlo methods to bridge the gap between stepwise ray marching and free-flight sampling [S4], and training-time optimizations like indicator grids for neural radiance fields to accelerate inference [S1]. Coarse cone tracing represents an earlier approach to accelerating SDF ray marching by leveraging pre-pass data [S5, unofficial].
+Ray marching optimization has evolved from basic traversal logic to sophisticated shader-level and data-structure-driven techniques. Early optimizations focused on traversal order, such as the voxel ray marching with traversal order optimization which minimizes floating-point operations by precomputing step deltas [S2]. With the rise of GPU computing, memory management became a focus, leading to tiled ray marching with grid sampling to handle large volumes [S1]. In real-time rendering, particularly for volumetric clouds, optimization shifted to material evaluation costs, introducing conservative density evaluation and grayscale material simplifications [S1]. More recent approaches exploit geometric coherence, such as epipolar sampling with 1D min/max binary trees for light shafts [S1], and training-based optimizations like indicator grids for deformable tissues [S1]. Monte Carlo methods have also seen adaptations, such as view-dependent visibility optimization to handle free-flight sampling [S4], and SDF-based optimizations like coarse cone tracing in games like Claybook [S5, unofficial].
 
